Limit one costly pixel shader to a single search step.

A fragment shader from a mesh painting plugin fills empty texels of a baked
position texture from the nearest painted one. It searches eight directions in
a loop with no early exit, so every empty texel runs all of it. Games run it
five to seven times in a row; each run took 3.6 ms on an Apple M4, which is more
than a frame at 72 Hz and shows up as constant micro-stutter at any resolution.

Stop the loop after one iteration for this shader only, identified by its
bytecode hash. Measured in the game: such frames fell from 1534 to 24 in about
ten minutes, and the painted result looks the same. The variant digest changes
for this shader alone, so no other shader cache entry is invalidated.
